114| \begin{example}[Overload: the polite stampede]\label{ex:g7:short-circuits-safety:overload}
115| Not every dangerous rush is a short circuit. A multi-socket
116| feeding a heater, a kettle and a toaster at once asks its one
117| branch to carry three full marches together --- each appliance
118| innocent, their sum an overload that warms the wall wiring like
119| a slow toaster. The breaker trips on the sum, and rightly so.
120| House rule: one hungry heat-making appliance per socket; the
121| multi-socket is for lamps and chargers, not for a private
122| kitchen.
123| \end{example}
124| 
125| \begin{remark}[The forbidden repair]\label{rem:g7:short-circuits-safety:nail}
126| The old story is true and still kills: a household's fuse keeps
127| blowing, and someone ``repairs'' it with a nail or a coin ---
128| a stout conductor that will never melt. The symptom is silenced;
129| the disease rages on. The next stampede meets no checkpoint, and
130| the house's own wires become the fuse --- inside the walls.
131| Never bypass a guard: a fuse that keeps blowing is a guard that
132| keeps telling the truth.
133| \end{remark}
134| 
135| \begin{remark}[The rules, final form]\label{rem:g7:short-circuits-safety:rules}
136| The family's electrical law-book, with its reasons now complete:
137| never bridge a battery's terminals, and keep spanners off car
138| batteries (a battery short in metal is a welding arc); one
139| heat-hungry appliance per socket (overload); damaged cords
140| retired (a fraying sleeve is a short circuit rehearsing); water
141| and mains apart, dry hands always (your body must never be the
142| bypass); guards respected --- matching fuses only, faults found
143| before resetting; and the socket itself, forever, not for
144| fingers or experiments.
145| \end{remark}
