149| \begin{exercise}[$\star$]\label{exo:g7:short-circuits-safety:1}
150| State the effortless-road proposition. Why does a bare wire win
151| the march away from a lamp?
152| \end{exercise}
153| 
154| \begin{exercise}[$\star$]\label{exo:g7:short-circuits-safety:2}
155| Distinguish the short circuit of a device from the short circuit
156| of the battery. Which is the graver, and why?
157| \end{exercise}
158| 
159| \begin{exercise}[$\star$]\label{exo:g7:short-circuits-safety:3}
160| In the safe demonstration, why does bulb 2 die --- and why does
161| bulb 1 brighten at the same moment?
162| \end{exercise}
163| 
164| \begin{exercise}[$\star$]\label{exo:g7:short-circuits-safety:4}
165| Why does a short circuit heat its wire? What everyday device
166| uses the same warming on purpose?
167| \end{exercise}
168| 
169| \begin{exercise}[$\star$]\label{exo:g7:short-circuits-safety:5}
170| What is a fuse, and what does it sacrifice? Why must it sit in
171| series with what it guards?
172| \end{exercise}
173| 
174| \begin{exercise}[$\star$]\label{exo:g7:short-circuits-safety:6}
175| How does a circuit breaker improve on the fuse? What must happen
176| before either guard is reset or replaced?
177| \end{exercise}
178| 
179| \begin{exercise}[$\star$]\label{exo:g7:short-circuits-safety:7}
180| A heater, kettle and toaster share one multi-socket. No wire is
181| bare, no device faulty --- yet the breaker trips. Name the event
182| and explain the branch's complaint.
183| \end{exercise}
184| 
185| \begin{exercise}[$\star$]\label{exo:g7:short-circuits-safety:8}
186| Why is ``repairing'' a fuse with a nail among the most dangerous
187| acts in a house? What becomes the fuse afterward?
188| \end{exercise}
189| 
190| \begin{exercise}[$\star\star$]\label{exo:g7:short-circuits-safety:9}
191| A cord's two inner wires, their sleeves frayed, touch each other
192| inside the cable --- upstream of the lamp they feed. Diagnose:
193| which kind of short circuit, what happens to the lamp, and what
194| does the branch's guard do?
195| \end{exercise}
196| 
197| \begin{exercise}[$\star\star$]\label{exo:g7:short-circuits-safety:10}
198| In the two-bulb demonstration, predict what happens if the
199| bypass wire is instead laid across \emph{both} bulbs together
200| --- from before bulb 1 to after bulb 2. Which definition now
201| applies, and why must the experimenter not try it?
202| \end{exercise}
